    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A fortified settlement stood at the centre of what later became Hanoi from the middle of the fifth century CE, under the names Tống Bình, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Lý Thái Tổ</a> made it the capital of Đại Việt in 1010.</li><li>The city kept its status under the Lý and the Lê, lost it to Huế in 1802, was renamed Hà Nội in 1831, and became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> in 1902.</li><li>It was the capital of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 1945 and of North Vietnam from 1954, and in 1976 it became the capital of the unified Socialist Republic of Vietnam.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Lý Thái Tổ</a> named his new capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, 'ascending dragon', after claiming to have seen a dragon ascending the Red River, and Hanoi's older names such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-bien">Long Biên</a> and Long Đỗ also featured the dragon.</p></aside>
    </section>

